*  DOMDEC_GPU test: CPU DOMDEC vs GPU DOMDEC for DHFR in a cubic box of
*  solvent using PBC w/ PME.  Total system contains 23,558 atoms, 21,069
*  atoms associated with water molecules and 2589 protein atoms.
*

! PART 1 compares the CPU-DOMDEC and GPU-DOMDEC energies and forces at
! one and the same geometry, with no SHAKE and nothing else in between,
! so the two sets of numbers are directly comparable.  PART 2 runs the
! same short dynamics down both paths, with SHAKE on for both, and
! compares the two paths again at each trajectory's final geometry.
!
! The GPU force terms are evaluated in single precision, so agreement is
! expected only to roundoff.  Measured spreads on an RTX 5000 Ada
! (2026-08, master b328d39ae) were, relative:
!
!    total energy 2.1e-07   VDW 7.3e-07   ELEC 7.8e-08
!    BOND 4.5e-07   ANGL 3.1e-08   RMS force 3.4e-05
!    10-step trajectory agreement 8.7e-08
!
! The tolerances below sit one to two orders above those, and three or
! more orders below anything a real DOMDEC_GPU error would produce.  A
! borderline failure should be judged against the numbers above rather
! than silently loosened.
!
! APH 2013.  Rewritten as a self-checking test 2026-08: this test used
! to end in an unconditional 'echo Test OK' and compared nothing at all.
! It also switched SHAKE on between the CPU and the GPU energy call, so
! the printed CPU energy was unSHAKEn while the printed GPU energy was
! SHAKEn.  The two differed by 741 kcal/mol, and that gap was read as a
! GPU failure when in fact nothing was wrong.  The old version wrote the
! CPU and GPU coordinates and forces to @9 and then never compared them.
! The comparisons are all done in the run now.  The four @9 files are
! still written, but note what they are: the coordinates and forces at
! the END of each PART 2 trajectory, not the PART 1 geometry the energy
! and force checks run on.

if ?domdec_g .ne. 1 then
   echo domdec_gpu TESTCASE RESULT: SKIP
   echo "Test NOT performed. DOMDEC_GPU must be defined in pref.dat"
   stop
endif

stream datadir.def

! Relative tolerances.  See the header for the measured spreads.
set etol   = 1e-5    ! energies, CPU vs GPU at the same geometry
set ftol   = 1e-3    ! RMS force difference / RMS force
! dyntol is deliberately much looser than the 8.7e-08 measured: it is the
! only check that compares two separately integrated trajectories rather
! than two evaluations of one geometry, so it is the one check that could
! drift with the GPU model.  It is a coarse "did the GPU integrator stay
! on the rails" test; the sharp checks are the same-geometry ones above
! and the two DYNA_*GEOM checks, which run at @etol.
set dyntol = 1e-3    ! CPU vs GPU trajectory after 10 steps

open unit 1 read form name @0/top_all22_prot.inp
read rtf card unit 1
close unit 1

open unit 1 read form name @0/par_all22_prot.inp
read param card unit 1
close unit 1

open unit 1 read form name @0/5dfr_minimized.crd
read sequ coor unit 1
close unit 1

generate 5dfr setup first nter last cter

read sequ tip3 7023
generate wat setup noangl nodihe

open unit 1 read form name @0/5dfr_solv.crd
read coor card unit 1
close unit 1

faster on

! Dimension of a box
Crystal define cubic 62.23 62.23 62.23 90.0 90.0 90.0
crystal build cutoff 11 noper 0

image byseg xcen 0.0 ycen 0.0 zcen 0.0 select segid 5dfr end
image byres xcen 0.0 ycen 0.0 zcen 0.0 select segid wat end

! enough digits that the comparisons below are not limited by printing
format (f20.10)

energy e14fac 1.5 eps 1.0 cutnb 11 cutim 11 ctofnb 9 ctonnb 7.5 vshi -
     Ewald kappa 0.320 pmEwald order 4 fftx 64 ffty 64 fftz 64

! =====================================================================
! PART 1: CPU DOMDEC vs GPU DOMDEC at the same geometry
!
! No SHAKE and no dynamics anywhere in this part.  Both calls see the
! coordinates just read from @0/5dfr_solv.crd, so every term must agree
! to single-precision roundoff.  The bonded terms are the useful tell:
! BOND and ANGL depend on nothing but the geometry, so if they disagree
! the two calls were not looking at the same structure.
! =====================================================================

energy domdec dlb off gpu off
set ecpu    = ?ener
set bondcpu = ?bond
set anglcpu = ?angl
set vdwcpu  = ?vdw
set eleccpu = ?elec

coor force comp
scalar xcomp store 1
scalar ycomp store 2
scalar zcomp store 3

! RMS of the per-atom CPU force magnitude, to make the force comparison
! below a relative one
scalar xcomp store 4
scalar xcomp *store 4
scalar ycomp store 5
scalar ycomp *store 5
scalar zcomp store 6
scalar zcomp *store 6
scalar 5 +store 4
scalar 6 +store 4
scalar wcomp recall 4
scalar wcomp stat
calc frmscpu = sqrt ( ?save )

energy domdec dlb off gpu on
set egpu    = ?ener
set bondgpu = ?bond
set anglgpu = ?angl
set vdwgpu  = ?vdw
set elecgpu = ?elec

echo ============================================================
echo PART 1: CPU DOMDEC vs GPU DOMDEC, same geometry, no SHAKE
echo ============================================================
echo   total  CPU @ecpu    GPU @egpu
echo   BOND   CPU @bondcpu GPU @bondgpu
echo   ANGL   CPU @anglcpu GPU @anglgpu
echo   VDW    CPU @vdwcpu  GPU @vdwgpu
echo   ELEC   CPU @eleccpu GPU @elecgpu

@testcheck @egpu    @ecpu    @etol DOMDEC_GPU_ETOT
@testcheck @bondgpu @bondcpu @etol DOMDEC_GPU_BOND
@testcheck @anglgpu @anglcpu @etol DOMDEC_GPU_ANGL
@testcheck @vdwgpu  @vdwcpu  @etol DOMDEC_GPU_VDW
@testcheck @elecgpu @eleccpu @etol DOMDEC_GPU_ELEC

! Forces too: a wrong GPU force with a right total energy is a real
! failure mode, and the energy checks above cannot see it.
! store 1,2,3 hold the CPU force; subtract it from the GPU force,
! square, sum, and average to get the RMS force difference.
coor force comp
scalar 1 mult -1.0
scalar 2 mult -1.0
scalar 3 mult -1.0
scalar xcomp +store 1
scalar ycomp +store 2
scalar zcomp +store 3
scalar 1 store 4
scalar 2 store 5
scalar 3 store 6
scalar 4 *store 1
scalar 5 *store 2
scalar 6 *store 3
scalar 3 +store 1
scalar 2 +store 1
scalar wcomp recall 1
scalar wcomp stat
calc dfrms = sqrt ( ?save )
calc frel  = @dfrms / @frmscpu

echo   RMS force CPU @frmscpu  RMS difference @dfrms  relative @frel

@testcheck @frel 0 @ftol DOMDEC_GPU_FORCE

! =====================================================================
! PART 2: short dynamics down both paths
!
! SHAKE is on for both runs and both start from the same coordinates, so
! the two trajectories are comparable.  After each run the final geometry
! is handed to the other path, which gives two more same-geometry CPU vs
! GPU energy comparisons -- this time on a structure that came out of
! dynamics, with the neighbour list rebuilt along the way.
! =====================================================================

shake fast bonh tol 1.0e-8 para

! ---- CPU DOMDEC dynamics ----
open unit 1 read form name @0/5dfr_solv.crd
read coor card unit 1
close unit 1

energy domdec dlb off gpu off

set flag = return_dyn1
goto run_dynamics
label return_dyn1

energy
set ecdyn = ?ener
write coor card name @9/cpu_xyz.crd
coor force comp
write coor comp card name @9/cpu_force.crd

! same geometry, other path
energy domdec dlb off gpu on
set ecdyng = ?ener

! ---- GPU DOMDEC dynamics, same starting coordinates ----
open unit 1 read form name @0/5dfr_solv.crd
read coor card unit 1
close unit 1

energy domdec dlb off gpu on

set flag = return_dyn2
goto run_dynamics
label return_dyn2

energy
set egdyn = ?ener
write coor card name @9/gpu_xyz.crd
coor force comp
write coor comp card name @9/gpu_force.crd

! same geometry, other path
energy domdec dlb off gpu off
set egdync = ?ener

echo ============================================================
echo PART 2: after 10 steps of dynamics, SHAKE on for both paths
echo ============================================================
echo   CPU-dynamics geometry: CPU @ecdyn  GPU @ecdyng
echo   GPU-dynamics geometry: GPU @egdyn  CPU @egdync

@testcheck @ecdyng @ecdyn  @etol   DOMDEC_GPU_DYNA_CPUGEOM
@testcheck @egdync @egdyn  @etol   DOMDEC_GPU_DYNA_GPUGEOM
@testcheck @egdyn  @ecdyn  @dyntol DOMDEC_GPU_DYNA_TRAJECTORY

! =====================================================================
! Verdict.  Each @testcheck above already prints its own
! '<label> TESTCASE RESULT: PASS|FAIL' line and bumps testfail, which is
! what the test harness scores on; this is the one-line summary.
! =====================================================================

format (i5)
incr testfail by 0        ! print the count as an integer, not 6.0000000000

echo ============================================================
echo domdec_gpu: @testfail check(s) failed
if @testfail .gt. 0 then
   echo domdec_gpu TESTCASE RESULT: FAIL
else
   echo domdec_gpu TESTCASE RESULT: PASS
endif

stop

! ************************************
! ******* Run dynamics ***************
! ************************************
! Called twice, once per path, so that both trajectories use exactly the
! same integrator settings.

label run_dynamics

! Run dynamics in periodic box
dynamics leap start timestep 0.002 nstep 10 nprint 1 iprfrq 1000 -
     iseed 123456 nsavc 0 nsavv 0 -
     firstt 298 finalt 298 twindl -5.0 twindh 5.0 -
     ichecw 1 teminc 30.0 ihtfrq 20 ieqfrq 100 -
     iasors 1 iasvel 1 iscvel 0  -
     inbfrq -1 imgfrq -1 ilbfrq 0 -
     eps 1.0 cutnb 11 cutim 11 ctofnb 9 ctonnb 7.5 vswi -
     Ewald kappa 0.320 pmEwald order 4 fftx 64 ffty 64 fftz 64 ntrfq 200 -
     hoover reft 298 tmass 250 tbath 298     ! Constant temperature

goto @flag
